nio: skip the pid-hash replace on the boot's first exec

Run 618's NULL deref was located by symbol lookup: pc 0xfffffff0076dae0c
is _phash_replace_locked, called from __mac_execve -> proc_exec_switch_task.
The chain is bsdinit_task -> load_init_program -> __mac_execve.

phash_replace_locked(old_proc, new_proc) replaces old_proc's entry in the
pid hash with new_proc.  On the boot thread old_proc is kernproc, a static
process that was never inserted into the pid hash, so its p_hash entry is
invalid and smr_hash_serialized_replace dereferences it -> data abort
far=0.  XNU only ever execs from a real hashed process (initproc).

The boot's first exec has no hashed old proc to replace.  Skip the replace
when current_task() is kernel_task; new_proc remains a valid process and
launchd can start.  Normal user execs still replace their hash entry.

diff --git a/bsd/kern/kern_exec.c b/bsd/kern/kern_exec.c
--- a/bsd/kern/kern_exec.c
+++ b/bsd/kern/kern_exec.c
@@ -4837,5 +4837,9 @@ proc_exec_switch_task(proc_t old_proc, proc_t new_proc, task_t old_task,
 
 		/* Switch proc in pid hash */
-		phash_replace_locked(old_proc, new_proc);
+		/* nio: boot's first exec has old_proc == kernproc, which is not
+		 * hashed; replacing its p_hash dereferences NULL. */
+		if (current_task() != kernel_task) {
+			phash_replace_locked(old_proc, new_proc);
+		}
 
 		/* Transfer the shadow flag to old proc */
